lesson_1 = "<div class=\"container\">\n\n<center>\n<img src=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/logo_datascientest.png\" style=\"height:150px\">\n</center>\n<hr style=\"border-color:#75DFC1; width:80%;\"/>\n<center><h1>Chapter 1: Introduction to Weights & Biases and Experiment Tracking</h1></center>\n<hr style=\"border-color:#75DFC1; width:80%;\"/>\n\n    <h2>a) Why experiment tracking is essential in MLOps</h2>\n\n<p>In Machine Learning Operations (MLOps), rigorous tracking of experiments is fundamental. Without it, it quickly becomes impossible to:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Understand performance</strong>: What hyperparameters, what data, what code produced a specific model?</li>\n<li><strong>Reproduce results</strong>: Essential for debugging, validation, and collaboration.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare approaches</strong>: How can we know if a new idea actually improves the model without a solid baseline for comparison?</li>\n<li><strong>Audit models</strong>: For compliance or governance reasons, it is often necessary to trace the origin and construction of a model.</li>\n<li><strong>Facilitate collaboration</strong>: Sharing experiences and their results allows teams to work more efficiently.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The monitoring of experiments brings a scientific discipline to the development of models, transforming what could be a chaotic process into a structured and iterative approach.</p>\n\n<h2>b) Presentation of the W&amp;B interface and its basic concepts (run, project, dashboard)</h2>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n   <img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/wandb.png\" style=\"width:50%\">\n</p>\n\n<p>Weights and Biases (also called W&amp;B) is a platform that simplifies the process of <strong>tracking</strong>, <strong>visualizing</strong>, and <strong>managing experiments</strong> in machine learning. It is used by teams responsible for model development to help them optimize their models and collaborate.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Basic Concepts:</strong></p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Run</strong> : A <code>run</code> corresponds to a single execution of a training script. Each run is associated with a set of hyperparameters, a trained model (potentially), a referenced dataset, and performance metrics. Each time you train a model with W&amp;B, a new <code>run</code> is created and its information is recorded.</li>\n<li><strong>Project</strong> : A <code>project</code> groups a set of <code>runs</code> related to the same problem or use case. For example, all experiments for a \"road accident classification\" model would be grouped in a single project. This allows for clear organization and easy comparison between different versions of models or configurations within this project.</li>\n<li><strong>Dashboard</strong> : The <code>dashboard</code> is the W&amp;B web interface where you can visualize the <code>runs</code> of your <code>projects</code>. It offers interactive graphs, customizable dashboards to analyze metrics, compare hyperparameters, inspect artifacts, and track system resource usage.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Here are some of the main features offered by Weights and Biases:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Experiment Tracking</strong>: W&amp;B allows you to record and track the details of each training experiment, including hyperparameters, performance metrics, and artifacts (such as model weights).</li>\n<li><strong>Interactive Visualizations</strong>: The platform offers a range of interactive graphs to visualize training results, making it easier to analyze model performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Collaboration and Reproducibility</strong>: Teams can share their experiments and collaborate more effectively, thus promoting the reproducibility of results.</li>\n<li><strong>Easy Integrations</strong>: W&amp;B seamlessly integrates with many popular machine learning libraries such as TensorFlow, PyTorch, and scikit-learn.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>c) Installation and configuration of W&amp;B in a Python environment</h2>\n\n<h3>Creation of the Weights and Biases Account</h3>\n\n<p>To use W&amp;B, it is necessary to <a href=\"https://wandb.ai/site/pricing\">create an account</a> and select \"Get Started\" on the free version.</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n   <img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/pricing.png\" style=\"width:100%\">\n</p>\n\n<p>The main difference between the free version of W&amp;B and the paid version lies in the amount of data that can be hosted on the tracking server (5GB vs 100GB for private artifacts). The use of the tool outside of this is significantly similar for the basic features.</p>\n\n<h3>Setting up the working environment</h3>\n\n<p>This course is based on the <a href=\"https://github.com/DataScientest-Studio/Template_MLOps_accidents\">github repository</a> of road accidents.</p>\n\n<p>If you have already cloned or forked it, go directly to the associated folder <code>Template_MLOps_accidents</code>. Otherwise, run the following commands:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-1\"><code class=\"language-bash\">git clone https://github.com/DataScientest-Studio/Template_MLOps_accidents.git\ncd Template_MLOps_accidents\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Feel free to take a look at the <code>README.md</code> file if you want more information about how this repository works.</p>\n\n<p>Then switch to the branch named <code>weights-and-biaises</code> of the repository.</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-0')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-0\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-3\"><code class=\"language-shell\">git checkout weights-and-biaises\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Before starting the course, make sure to set up your virtual environment.</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-2')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-2\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-5\"><code class=\"language-shell\">uv venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<h2>d) Installation of libraries and W&amp;B</h2>\n\n<p>You must now install all the necessary libraries for this module within your virtual environment, including the Weights &amp; Biases library <code>wandb</code>, which will be used for tracking experiments.</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-4')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-4\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-7\"><code class=\"language-shell\">uv pip install -r requirements.txt\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Then to connect:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-9\"><code class=\"language-shell\">wandb login\n</code></pre>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-11\"><code class=\"language-\">wandb: WARNING Using legacy-service, which is deprecated. If this is unintentional, you can fix it by ensuring you do not call `wandb.require('legacy-service')` and do not set the WANDB_X_REQUIRE_LEGACY_SERVICE environment variable.\nwandb: Logging into wandb.ai. (Learn how to deploy a W&B server locally: https://wandb.me/wandb-server)\nwandb: You can find your API key in your browser here: https://wandb.ai/authorize?ref=models \nwandb: Paste an API key from your profile and hit enter, or press ctrl+c to quit:\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>W&amp;B will then ask you for an \"API key\" that you can obtain by logging in at this <a href=\"https://wandb.ai/authorize\">link</a></p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n   <img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/api_key.png\" style=\"width:30%\">\n</p>\n\n<p>Paste this personal key into the terminal and press <strong><code>Enter</code></strong></p>\n\n<p>You are connected!</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-13\"><code class=\"language-\">wandb: No netrc file found, creating one.\nwandb: Appending key for api.wandb.ai to your netrc file: /home/datas/.netrc\nwandb: Currently logged in as: johan-l (datascientest-com) to https://api.wandb.ai. Use `wandb login --relogin` to force relogin\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Your environment is now ready to use Weights &amp; Biases!</p>\n\n</div>\n        <script src=\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.3.1.slim.min.js\"\n            integrity=\"sha384-q8i/X+965DzO0rT7abK41JStQIAqVgRVzpbzo5smXKp4YfRvH+8abtTE1Pi6jizo\"\n            crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>\n        \n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/prism.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-RDQSW3KoqJMiX0L/UBgwBmH1EmRYp8LBOiLaA8rBHIy+7OGP/7Gxg8vbt8wG4ZYd29P0Fnoq6+LOytCqx3cyoQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/themes/prism-okaidia.min.css\" integrity=\"sha512-mIs9kKbaw6JZFfSuo+MovjU+Ntggfoj8RwAmJbVXQ5mkAX5LlgETQEweFPI18humSPHymTb5iikEOKWF7I8ncQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"\n/>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/line-numbers/prism-line-numbers.min.css\" integrity=\"sha512-cbQXwDFK7lj2Fqfkuxbo5iD1dSbLlJGXGpfTDqbggqjHJeyzx88I3rfwjS38WJag/ihH7lzuGlGHpDBymLirZQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"\n    referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" />\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/autoloader/prism-autoloader.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-fTl/qcO1VgvKtOMApX2PdZzkziyr2stM65GYPLGuYMnuMm1z2JLJG6XVU7C/mR+E7xBUqCivykuhlzfqxXBXbg==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/line-numbers/prism-line-numbers.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-BttltKXFyWnGZQcRWj6osIg7lbizJchuAMotOkdLxHxwt/Hyo+cl47bZU0QADg+Qt5DJwni3SbYGXeGMB5cBcw==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<script src=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/de_custom.js\"></script>\n<link href=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/de_custom.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n",

lesson_2 = "<div class=\"container\">\n\n<center>\n<img src=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/logo_datascientest.png\" style=\"height:150px\">\n</center>\n<hr style=\"border-color:#75DFC1; width:80%;\"/>\n<center><h1>Chapter 2: Experiment Tracking and Hyperparameter Management</h1></center>\n<hr style=\"border-color:#75DFC1; width:80%;\"/>\n\n    <h2>Preface</h2>\n\n<p>This course is based on the <a href=\"https://github.com/DataScientest-Studio/Template_MLOps_accidents\">github repository</a> of road accidents.</p>\n\n<p>It is necessary to switch to the <code>weights-and-biases</code> branch of this repository.</p>\n\n<p>And activate your virtual environment that you created in the previous chapter.</p>\n\n<h1>1. Getting Started</h1>\n\n<h2>a) Data Preparation (Prerequisites)</h2>\n\n<p>Before moving on to the modeling stage, it is essential to start by importing the raw data and applying a first level of preprocessing.\nThis is done via a Python script already prepared in the <strong><code>src/data</code></strong> folder.</p>\n\n<p>Open the file <strong>import_raw_data.py</strong>, then locate the definition of the function <code>main</code>.\nBy default, it is written as follows:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-1\"><code class=\"language-python\">def main(raw_data_relative_path=\"../data/raw\", ...)\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Modify it to remove the <code>../</code> and ensure that the path is relative to the root of the project.</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-0')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-0\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-3\"><code class=\"language-python\">def main(raw_data_relative_path=\"data/raw\")\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Once the modification is made, you can run the script.</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-2')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-2\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-5\"><code class=\"language-shell\">python3 src/data/import_raw_data.py\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>This will load the raw data located in the <code>data/raw</code> folder.</p>\n\n<p>Once the raw data is imported, we can run the preprocessing script located in the file <strong>make_dataset.py</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-4')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-4\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-7\"><code class=\"language-shell\">python3 src/data/make_dataset.py\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>When executed, the script will ask you for two paths:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>input path</strong>: the folder where the raw data is located,</li>\n<li><strong>output path</strong>: the folder where the preprocessed data will be saved.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Then enter the following paths:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Input</strong> : data/raw</li>\n<li><strong>Output</strong> : data/preprocessed</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>b) Connection to Weights and Biases</h2>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-9\"><code class=\"language-shell\">wandb login\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>To connect to the Weights and Biases (W&amp;B) API, we will consistently use the command <code>wandb login</code>. This command provides you with a link to log into your W&amp;B account.</p>\n\n<p>You will find the API key that you need to paste into the window that appears on the screen to connect to it.</p>\n\n<p>As long as the terminal has not been reset, your session will remain active and you will be able to continue using W&amp;B without interruption. However, if you attempt to resume after a terminal reset (which can occur, for example, when the VM goes to sleep), you will need to rerun this cell and re-enter your API key.</p>\n\n<h2>c) Recording metrics, hyperparameters, and results (First Run)</h2>\n\n<p>Weights &amp; Biases (W&amp;B) is a tool for tracking and visualizing experiments in Machine Learning. It allows for automatic plotting of model training, comparing different runs, and effectively collaborating on Data Science projects.\nOne of the key concepts of W&amp;B is based on two fundamental entities: <strong>Runs</strong> and <strong>Projects</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>A run corresponds to a single execution of a training script. Each run is associated with:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>a set of hyperparameters</li>\n<li>a trained model</li>\n<li>a dataset</li>\n<li>performance metrics</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In other words, every time you train a model, W&amp;B logs that execution as a distinct run.</p>\n\n<p>A Project groups a set of runs around the same issue or use case. For example, if you are working on the classification of road accidents, all experiments related to this topic will be gathered in a single project. This allows for clear organization and easy comparison between different versions of models or configurations.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>Create a file <strong>first_run.py</strong> in the <strong><code>src/</code></strong> folder, then copy the following code:</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-11\"><code class=\"language-python\">import wandb\n\nrun = wandb.init(\n    project=\"classification-car-accidents\",\n    name='My first run',\n    tags=[\"baseline\", \"random-forest\"],\n)\n\nThis code initializes a W&B run. When this script is executed, W&B automatically creates a tracking environment.\n\nAt this stage, we will also integrate **Weights & Biases** (W&B) to track hyperparameters, model performance, and artifacts.\n\nIn your **first_run.py** file, define the model hyperparameters as a dictionary:\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python</p>\n\n<h1>Define the hyperparameters</h1>\n\n<p>params = {\"n_estimators\": 100, \"criterion\": \"gini\", \"max_depth\": 10}</p>\n\n<h1>Save the hyperparameters in W&amp;B</h1>\n\n<p>wandb.config = params</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-13\"><code class=\"language-\">\n&gt; This allows you to keep track of the hyperparameters used for each training, which is essential for reproducibility and comparative analysis.\n\nThen, use these parameters to instantiate the model:\n\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport pandas as pd \nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nimport numpy as np</p>\n\n<p>X_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_train.csv')\nX_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_test.csv')\ny_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_train.csv')\ny_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_test.csv')\ny_train = np.ravel(y_train)\ny_test = np.ravel(y_test)</p>\n\n<p>rf_classifier = RandomForestClassifier(**params)</p>\n\n<p>rf_classifier.fit(X_train, y_train)</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-15\"><code class=\"language-\">\nOnce the model is trained, we can evaluate its performance on the training and test sets, and then save it in W&B using `wandb.log()`:\n\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\ntrain_accuracy = rf_classifier.score(X_train, y_train)\ntest_accuracy = rf_classifier.score(X_test, y_test)\nwandb.log({\"train_accuracy\": train_accuracy, \"test_accuracy\": test_accuracy})</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-17\"><code class=\"language-\">\n&gt; The metrics will be visible in the W&B web interface, making their analysis and comparison easier.\n\nIt is possible (and recommended) to save the trained model as an artifact, so that it can be reused or deployed later. W&B also allows for versioning these artifacts.\n\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport joblib</p>\n\n<h1>Save the model locally</h1>\n\n<p>model_filename = './src/models/trained_model.joblib'\njoblib.dump(rf_classifier, model_filename)\nprint(\"Model trained and saved successfully.\")</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-19\"><code class=\"language-\">\nIt is important to explicitly close the W&B run to properly finalize the recording of all associated metrics, artifacts, and logs:\n\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport time</p>\n\n<p>time.sleep(60)\nwandb.finish()</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-21\"><code class=\"language-\">\nThe line `time.sleep(60)` allows for a voluntary pause of 60 seconds before closing the W&B session. This step may seem optional, but it is particularly useful when you have enabled automatic capture of system resources (CPU, RAM, GPU, etc.) or when you are logging relatively large artifacts. This short wait ensures that all information has been properly synchronized with the W&B online dashboard, thus avoiding any data loss or incomplete metrics. [For more information](https://docs.wandb.ai/guides/models/app/settings-page/system-metrics/)\n\nFinally, the call to `wandb.finish()` properly closes the current run. This prevents any subsequent metrics from being mistakenly attached to the same session, which is crucial for ensuring the clarity, consistency, and reproducibility of your experiments.\n\nExecute the file :\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-6')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-6\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>shell\npython3 src/first_run.py    </p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-23\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\n\n**first_run.py** (Complete solution) : \n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-8')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-8\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport os\nimport wandb\nimport pandas as pd \nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nimport numpy as np\nimport joblib</p>\n\n<p>run = wandb.init(\n    project=\"classification-car-accidents\",\n    name='My first run',\n    tags=[\"baseline\", \"random-forest\"],\n)</p>\n\n<p>params = {\"n_estimators\": 2, \"criterion\": 'gini', \"max_depth\": 2}</p>\n\n<p>wandb.config = params</p>\n\n<p>X_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_train.csv')\nX_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_test.csv')\ny_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_train.csv')\ny_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_test.csv')\ny_train = np.ravel(y_train)\ny_test = np.ravel(y_test)</p>\n\n<p>rf_classifier = RandomForestClassifier(**params)</p>\n\n<p>rf_classifier.fit(X_train, y_train)</p>\n\n<p>train_accuracy = rf_classifier.score(X_train, y_train)\ntest_accuracy = rf_classifier.score(X_test, y_test)\nwandb.log({\"train_accuracy\": train_accuracy, \"test_accuracy\": test_accuracy})</p>\n\n<h1>Save the trained model to a file</h1>\n\n<p>model_filename = 'models/trained_model.joblib'\njoblib.dump(rf_classifier, model_filename)</p>\n\n<p>time.sleep(60)</p>\n\n<p>wandb.finish()</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-25\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nExample of terminal output:\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>txt\nwandb: Currently logged in as: johan-l (datascientest-com) to https://api.wandb.ai. Use <code>wandb login --relogin</code> to force relogin\nwandb: Tracking run with wandb version 0.19.11\nwandb: Run data is saved locally in /mnt/c/Users/datas/Documents/Johan/WandB/Template_MLOps_accidents/wandb/run-20250520_171342-xj27e121\nwandb: Run <code>wandb offline</code> to turn off syncing.\nwandb: Syncing run My first run\nwandb: \u2b50\ufe0f View project at https://wandb.ai/datascientest-com/classification-car-accidents\nwandb: \ud83d\ude80 View run at https://wandb.ai/datascientest-com/classification-car-accidents/runs/xj27e121\nwandb:\nwandb:\nwandb: Run history:\nwandb:  test_accuracy \u2581\nwandb: train_accuracy \u2581\nwandb:\nwandb: Run summary:\nwandb:  test_accuracy 0.71834\nwandb: train_accuracy 0.72064\nwandb:\nwandb: \ud83d\ude80 View run My first run at: https://wandb.ai/datascientest-com/classification-car-accidents/runs/xj27e121\nwandb: \u2b50\ufe0f View project at: https://wandb.ai/datascientest-com/classification-car-accidents\nwandb: Synced 5 W&amp;B file(s), 0 media file(s), 2 artifact file(s) and 0 other file(s)\nwandb: Find logs at: ./wandb/run-20250520_171342-xj27e121/logs</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-27\"><code class=\"language-\">\nAfter execution, a **`wandb`** folder is created at the root of your project. It contains all the data related to your runs, with a typical structure like:\n\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>\u251c\u2500\u2500 wandb \n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 run-DATE_ID\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 files\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 logs\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 tmp</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-29\"><code class=\"language-\">\n**run-DATE_ID** : This folder corresponds to a unique execution (or run), identified by a date and a unique identifier. It contains all the artifacts and metadata associated with this run.\n\n**files/** : Contains the configuration and tracking files generated automatically or manually during the run, including:\n\n- **config.yaml** : the hyperparameters and other configuration variables used in the run.\n- **output.log** : the standard outputs (logs, print, etc.) generated by your script.\n- **wandb-metadata.json** : the environment metadata (machine name, Python version, etc.).\n- **requirements.txt** : the dependencies of the environment at the time of execution (if wandb was configured to capture them).\n- Model files or any other files that you manually logged with wandb.log_artifact().\n\n**logs/** : Contains the internal system logs used by W&B to track the performance of the run :\n\n- Information on CPU/GPU usage.\n- Timestamps of events during execution.\n- Details on communication between the local client and the W&B server (useful for debugging).\n\n**tmp/** : Temporary directory used by W&B to store intermediate files before synchronization with W&B servers:\n\n- Buffered data waiting to be loaded.\n- Graphs or artifacts not yet transferred.\n- Various cache folders.\n\n&gt; This local folder is comparable to the **`mlruns`** folder used by MLflow. It acts as a local cache that stores the information that W&B then synchronizes with your online account.\n\n## c) Visualization of results\n\nThe run we just performed is now complete. We can go see the results on the [site](https://wandb.ai/home).\n\nAfter logging in, you will be able to select your project `classification-car-accidents` in `My Projects`.\n\n&lt;p align=\"center\"&gt;\n   &lt;img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/my_project.png\" style=\"width:100%\"&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;\n\nYou will finally be able to select your run `My first run` from the list that appears on the left.\n\n&lt;p align=\"center\"&gt;\n   &lt;img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/my_first_run.png\" style=\"width:100%\"&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;\n\n\nWe will see in more detail how to use this interface in the rest of this course. Now that you understand how to perform a run, we will be able to carry out others.\n\n&lt;br&gt;\n\n# 2. Visualization of metrics\n\n## a) Second Run W&B\n\nBefore discovering the visualization tools offered by Weights & Biases, it is useful to generate a second run with a different model. This will allow for comparing the performances of several models in the W&B interface.\n\nIn this second experiment, we will train a decision tree (**DecisionTreeClassifier** from `scikit-learn`) using the following parameters:\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\n{\"criterion\": \"gini\", \"max_depth\": 10}</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-31\"><code class=\"language-\">\nThe first 3 steps to follow are the same as before:\n\n- Initialization of a new W&B run\n- Recording hyperparameters in the config\n- Training the model\n\n&gt; Create a new python file **second_run.py** in the **`src/`** folder and add the 3 steps above.\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-10')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-10\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport os\nimport wandb\nimport pandas as pd \nimport numpy as np\nimport joblib\nimport time\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier</p>\n\n<h1>1. Start a W&amp;B Run</h1>\n\n<p>run = wandb.init(\n    project=\"classification-car-accidents\",\n    name='My second run',\n    tags=[\"baseline\", \"Decision Tree\"],\n)</p>\n\n<h1>\u20032. Capture a dictionary of hyperparameters</h1>\n\n<p>params = {\"criterion\": 'gini', \"max_depth\": 10}</p>\n\n<p>wandb.config = params</p>\n\n<p>X_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_train.csv')\nX_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_test.csv')\ny_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_train.csv')\ny_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_test.csv')\ny_train = np.ravel(y_train)\ny_test = np.ravel(y_test)</p>\n\n<h1>3. Train the model</h1>\n\n<p>dt_classifier = DecisionTreeClassifier(**params)\ndt_classifier.fit(X_train, y_train)</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-33\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nTo effectively compare multiple models on the Weights & Biases interface, it is essential to log **the same metric names** in each run. This allows W&B to automatically display comparative curves in the dashboard.\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-12')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-12\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python</p>\n\n<h1>\u20034. Capture a dictionary of metrics</h1>\n\n<p>train_accuracy = dt_classifier.score(X_train, y_train)\ntest_accuracy = dt_classifier.score(X_test, y_test)\nwandb.log({\"train_accuracy\": train_accuracy, \"test_accuracy\": test_accuracy})</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-35\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nNow, we will take advantage of the fact that our model comes from `scikit-learn` to use the integration of `scikit-learn` in the W&B library (you can find the references for the functions used [here](https://docs.wandb.ai/guides/integrations/scikit#example)).\n\nWhen using a classification model from `scikit-learn`, you can use the function `wandb.sklearn.plot_classifier` which allows you to automatically track graphs traditionally used for result analysis in addition to the usual metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, f1-score).\n\nThis function requires a few specific parameters such as the probabilities estimated by the model (which we will call `y_probas` here) and the names of the features.\n\nAs before, once the run is completed, do not forget to close it (with the function `wandb.finish()`)\n\nAdd the following code and observe the results obtained for the run **My second run** in the W&B UI (loading the graphs may take a few minutes):  \n\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python</p>\n\n<h1>5. Track plots with sklearn.plot_classifier</h1>\n\n<p>y_pred = dt_classifier.predict(X_test)\ny_probas = dt_classifier.predict_proba(X_test)\nlabels = ['non-prioritary accident', 'prioritary accident']</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-37\"><code class=\"language-\">\nwandb.sklearn.plot_classifier(\n    dt_classifier,\n    X_train,\n    X_test,\n    y_train,\n    y_test,\n    y_pred,\n    y_probas,\n    labels,\n    model_name=\"Decision Tree\",\n    feature_names=X_train.columns,\n)\n\nAs with each execution, it is recommended to properly close the run by adding a delay of about 60 seconds `time.sleep(60)`. This delay allows W&B to finalize the collection of system metrics before closing the session. Finally, end the run with the method `wandb.finish()` to correctly save and synchronize all tracked data.\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-14')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-14\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport time</p>\n\n<p>time.sleep(60)</p>\n\n<h1>Finish the run</h1>\n\n<p>wandb.finish()</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-39\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nExecute the file :\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-16')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-16\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>shell\npython3 src/second_run.py   </p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-41\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nWhile navigating the Weights & Biases (W&B) interface, you can find different sections:\n\n- **Custom Charts** : This section allows you to create and customize your own visualizations from the metrics you have logged with `wandb.log()` (e.g., train_accuracy, test_loss, etc.). You can also choose the type of chart (lines, histograms, bars, etc.).\n- **Media** : to visualize the graphical outputs you log via `wandb.log()`.\n- **Tables** : to display tabular data, often derived from datasets or predictions.\n- **Charts** : to visualize the tracking curves (loss, accuracy, etc.) generated by calls to `wandb.log()` during training.\n- **System** : allows you to collect and visualize system metrics associated with model training (CPU usage, GPU, RAM memory, etc.). These metrics are automatically recorded by W&B at a frequency of about every 15 seconds, which justifies the addition of a delay `time.sleep(60)` to allow sufficient time for their collection and display.\n\n\n## b) Compare the runs with each other\n\nBefore we can compare our different runs, we will generate a third one (**third_run.py**). To do this, simply follow the same steps as those taken during the second run:\n\n- Initialize a new W&B run\n- Log the hyperparameters in the `config`\n- Train the model with these parameters\n- Track the performance with `wandb.sklearn.plot_classifier`\n\nThe only difference: this time we will use different hyperparameters to explore another configuration of the model.\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-18')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-18\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>python\nimport os\nimport wandb\nimport pandas as pd \nimport numpy as np\nimport joblib\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier</p>\n\n<h1>1. Start a W&amp;B Run</h1>\n\n<p>run = wandb.init(\n    project=\"classification-car-accidents\",\n    name='My third run',\n    tags=[\"baseline\", \"Decision Tree\"],\n)</p>\n\n<h1>2. Capture a dictionary of hyperparameters</h1>\n\n<p>params = {\"criterion\": 'entropy', \"max_depth\": 20}</p>\n\n<p>wandb.config = params</p>\n\n<p>X_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_train.csv')\nX_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/X_test.csv')\ny_train = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_train.csv')\ny_test = pd.read_csv('data/preprocessed/y_test.csv')\ny_train = np.ravel(y_train)\ny_test = np.ravel(y_test)</p>\n\n<h1>3. Train the model</h1>\n\n<p>dt_classifier = DecisionTreeClassifier(**params)\ndt_classifier.fit(X_train, y_train)</p>\n\n<h1>\u20034. Capture a dictionary of metrics</h1>\n\n<p>train_accuracy = dt_classifier.score(X_train, y_train)\ntest_accuracy = dt_classifier.score(X_test, y_test)\nwandb.log({\"train_accuracy\": train_accuracy, \"test_accuracy\": test_accuracy})</p>\n\n<h1>5. Track plots with sklearn.plot_classifier</h1>\n\n<p>y_pred = dt_classifier.predict(X_test)\ny_probas = dt_classifier.predict_proba(X_test)\nlabels = ['non-prioritary accident', 'prioritary accident']</p>\n\n<p>wandb.sklearn.plot_classifier(\n    dt_classifier,\n    X_train,\n    X_test,\n    y_train,\n    y_test,\n    y_pred,\n    y_probas,\n    labels,\n    model_name=\"Decision Tree\",\n    feature_names=X_train.columns,\n)</p>\n\n<p>time.sleep(60)</p>\n\n<h1>6. Finish the run</h1>\n\n<p>wandb.finish()</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-43\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nExecute the file :\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-20')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-20\" style=\"display:none\">\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>shell\npython3 src/third_run.py    </p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-45\"><code class=\"language-\">\n\n</div>\n\n\nPosition yourself at the level of the project `classification-car-accidents`.\n\nThe results of the three runs are now displayed in the same graphs. Hence the importance of always giving the same name to the metrics that represent the same information in 2 different runs (otherwise we would not be able to visualize them this way).\n\n&gt; Based on the visualizations you were able to obtain here, which model seems to be the most effective?\n\n\n<center>\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-22')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n</center>\n<div id=\"solution-22\" style=\"display:none\">\n<p>&lt;p align=\"center\"&gt;\n   &lt;img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/run_comparaison.png\" style=\"width:70%\"&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;</p>\n\n<p>In our case, the model from the second run achieves a slightly higher accuracy on the test set and therefore seems to be more effective.</p>\n\n</div>\n\n\nYou may have noticed that the first run does not appear in the `Media` category graphs because we did not use the built-in scikit-learn function `plot_classifier`.\n\nHowever, the \"overlaid\" display of the graphs in the `Media` category does not really allow for a comparison of runs 2 and 3, and some of these graphs are sometimes unreadable. Furthermore, the graphs in this category, unlike those in the `Charts` category, cannot be modified.\n\n&lt;p align=\"center\"&gt;\n   &lt;img src=\"https://datascientest-mlops.s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/mlops_weight_nd_biases/comparaison_feature_importance.png\" style=\"width:70%\"&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;\n\nIt is therefore recommended to track all useful metrics for model comparison (accuracy, f1-score, precision, recall...) and specific run information directly via the method `wandb.log` in addition to what the function `plot_classifier` (or equivalent) does.\n\nIn this chapter, we explored the basic features of Weights & Biases (W&B) for tracking experiments in Machine Learning. We learned to:\n\n- Initialize a W&B run for each model training.\n- Log the hyperparameters used to ensure reproducibility.\n- Track performance metrics (accuracy, etc.) and visualize them in the W&B interface.\n- Use W&B integrations (like the one with scikit-learn) to generate specific analysis plots.\n- Compare the results of different runs directly in the W&B dashboard.\n- Understand the structure of the `wandb` folder generated locally.\n\nThese steps form the foundation of effective experiment management, allowing for an organized record of each attempt, easy comparison of approaches, and identification of the most successful configurations.</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n        <script src=\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.3.1.slim.min.js\"\n            integrity=\"sha384-q8i/X+965DzO0rT7abK41JStQIAqVgRVzpbzo5smXKp4YfRvH+8abtTE1Pi6jizo\"\n            crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>\n        \n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/prism.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-RDQSW3KoqJMiX0L/UBgwBmH1EmRYp8LBOiLaA8rBHIy+7OGP/7Gxg8vbt8wG4ZYd29P0Fnoq6+LOytCqx3cyoQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/themes/prism-okaidia.min.css\" integrity=\"sha512-mIs9kKbaw6JZFfSuo+MovjU+Ntggfoj8RwAmJbVXQ5mkAX5LlgETQEweFPI18humSPHymTb5iikEOKWF7I8ncQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"\n/>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/line-numbers/prism-line-numbers.min.css\" integrity=\"sha512-cbQXwDFK7lj2Fqfkuxbo5iD1dSbLlJGXGpfTDqbggqjHJeyzx88I3rfwjS38WJag/ihH7lzuGlGHpDBymLirZQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"\n    referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" />\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/autoloader/prism-autoloader.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-fTl/qcO1VgvKtOMApX2PdZzkziyr2stM65GYPLGuYMnuMm1z2JLJG6XVU7C/mR+E7xBUqCivykuhlzfqxXBXbg==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/line-numbers/prism-line-numbers.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-BttltKXFyWnGZQcRWj6osIg7lbizJchuAMotOkdLxHxwt/Hyo+cl47bZU0QADg+Qt5DJwni3SbYGXeGMB5cBcw==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<script src=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/de_custom.js\"></script>\n<link href=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/de_custom.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n",

lesson_3 = "<div class=\"container\">\n\n<center>\n<img src=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/logo_datascientest.png\" style=\"height:150px\">\n</center>\n<hr style=\"border-color:#75DFC1; width:80%;\"/>\n<center><h1>Chapter 3: Artifact Management and Reproducibility</h1></center>\n<hr style=\"border-color:#75DFC1; width:80%;\"/>\n\n    <h2>Preface</h2>\n\n<p>This course is based on the <a href=\"https://github.com/DataScientest-Studio/Template_MLOps_accidents\">github repository</a> of road accidents.</p>\n\n<p>It is necessary to switch to the <code>weights-and-biases</code> branch of this repository.</p>\n\n<p>Activate your virtual environment that you created in the previous chapter and connect to Wandb.</p>\n\n<h1>1. The artifacts</h1>\n\n<h2>a) What is an artifact in W&amp;B?</h2>\n\n<p>In Weights &amp; Biases, an artifact is a way to version and track any file or set of files produced or used during your machine learning experiments. This can include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Datasets</strong>: raw, preprocessed, training/validation/test sets</li>\n<li><strong>Models</strong>: weights of trained models, model architecture</li>\n<li><strong>Results</strong>: prediction files, result graphs, reports</li>\n<li><strong>Code</strong>: snapshots of your training code</li>\n<li><strong>Configurations</strong>: important configuration files</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Artifacts allow for the creation of data lineage, ensure that experiments are reproducible, and facilitate deployment by providing stable and identifiable versions of models and data. Each artifact is versioned, which means you can revert to previous versions and understand how changes have affected your results.</p>\n\n<h2>b) Backup and versioning of datasets, models, and results</h2>\n\n<p>Let's take the example of the script <strong>second_run.py</strong> presented in the previous chapter and modify it in an academic and reproducible framework to save the trained model as well as the training and test datasets as W&amp;B artifacts.</p>\n\n<p>This approach allows for:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Ensure the traceability of experiments,</li>\n<li>Facilitate the sharing of results,</li>\n<li>Guarantee the reproducibility of training through versioning of artifacts.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>First, rename your run to <code>My second run - With Artifacts</code>. This explicit name will facilitate the identification of this experiment in the Weights &amp; Biases (W&amp;B) project tracking interface, particularly by indicating that it includes artifact logging (model and datasets).</p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-0')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-0\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-1\"><code class=\"language-python\">run = wandb.init(\n    project=\"classification-car-accidents\",\n    name='My second run - With Artifacts',\n    tags=[\"baseline\", \"Decision Tree\"],\n)\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>Before you can save a model as an artifact in W&amp;B, you first need to save it as a file on disk. This allows you to have a physical version of the model that you can later load, share, or version. To do this, we use the <code>joblib</code> library, which is very efficient for serializing complex Python objects like scikit-learn models.</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-3\"><code class=\"language-python\">os.makedirs(\"models\", exist_ok=True)\nmodel_path = \"models/dt_model.joblib\"\njoblib.dump(dt_classifier, model_path)\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Finally, W&amp;B offers a simple API to log any file or folder as an artifact. We use the following three functions for this:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li><code>wandb.Artifact()</code>\nThis function allows you to create an artifact locally, specifying:</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li>name: name of the artifact,</li>\n<li>type: type of resource (\"model\" or \"dataset\" here),</li>\n<li>description: to describe the content.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-5\"><code class=\"language-python\">artifact_name = wandb.Artifact(\n    name=\"example_model\",\n    type=\"model\",\n    description=\"example_model_description\"\n)\n</code></pre>\n\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li>.add_file()\nThis method allows you to add one or more files to the artifact using their local path.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-7\"><code class=\"language-python\">model_artifact.add_file(\"PATH_TO_MODEL_FILE\")\n</code></pre>\n\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>wandb.log_artifact()\nThis command actually saves the artifact in the active W&amp;B run, making it visible and versioned in the interface.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-9\"><code class=\"language-python\">wandb.log_artifact(artifact_name)\n</code></pre>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>Modify the script <strong>second_run.py</strong> so that it saves both the trained model and the training and test datasets as W&amp;B artifacts. This configuration will allow for precise tracking of the origin of the obtained results, facilitate sharing, and ensure the reproducibility of experiments.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>We start by logging the files containing the training and test splits as an artifact of type <code>dataset</code>. Add the following code after the section <code># \u20032. Capture a dictionary of hyperparameters</code> in the script:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-11\"><code class=\"language-python\"># Log datasets as artifact\ndata_artifact = wandb.Artifact(\n    name=\"car-accident-dataset\",\n    type=\"dataset\",\n    description=\"Train/test splits used for second run\"\n)\ndata_artifact.add_file(\"data/preprocessed/X_train.csv\")\ndata_artifact.add_file(\"data/preprocessed/X_test.csv\")\ndata_artifact.add_file(\"data/preprocessed/y_train.csv\")\ndata_artifact.add_file(\"data/preprocessed/y_test.csv\")\nwandb.log_artifact(data_artifact)\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Then, we save the trained model locally, and then we log it as an artifact of type <code>model</code> with a precise description. Add the following code after the <code># 3. Train the model</code> section of the script:</p>\n\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-13\"><code class=\"language-python\"># Save model locally and create W&B artifact\nos.makedirs(\"models\", exist_ok=True)\nmodel_path = \"models/dt_model.joblib\"\njoblib.dump(dt_classifier, model_path)\n\nmodel_artifact = wandb.Artifact(\n    name=\"decision_tree_model\",\n    type=\"model\",\n    description=\"Decision Tree trained with gini and max_depth=10\",\n)\n\nmodel_artifact.add_file(model_path)\nwandb.log_artifact(model_artifact)\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>Execute the script : </p>\n\n<p><center></p>\n\n<div class=\"btn btn-solution btn-default\" onclick=\"show_hide('solution-2')\" style=\"background-color:#484a69; color:white\">\nShow / Hide solution\n</div>\n\n<p></center></p>\n\n<div id=\"solution-2\" style=\"display:none\">\n<pre class=\"line-numbers\" id=\"code-15\"><code class=\"language-shell\">python3 second_run.py\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n<p>At the end of the execution of the modified script, go to the W&amp;B project dashboard. A new section titled \"Artifacts\" will appear in the left sidebar menu. This section groups all the files logged as artifacts during the run, including:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>The trained model (decision-tree-model),</li>\n<li>The training dataset (X_train and y_train),</li>\n<li>The test dataset (X_test and y_test).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Each artifact is associated with an explicit name and automatically receives a version (for example v0).</p>\n\n<p>When this script, or another using the same artifact name, is relaunched:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>If the content of the files has been modified</strong>, a new version (e.g., v1, v2, etc.) of the artifact is automatically generated.</li>\n<li><strong>If the content is identical</strong>, W&amp;B does not create a new version but reuses the existing version (thus optimizing experiment tracking).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>This integrated version management by W&amp;B ensures fine and reproducible traceability of the artifacts used throughout the experiments.</p>\n\n<h1>2. Reproducibility</h1>\n\n<h2>a) Good practices for experiments</h2>\n\n<p>When conducting an experiment in machine learning, particularly with logging via Weights &amp; Biases (W&amp;B), certain key steps must be systematically followed. Here is a practical methodology to follow during each run:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Clean and descriptive initialization of the run\nAlways start the run via <code>wandb.init()</code> with:</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li>An explicit name (<code>name=\"Run RF max_depth=10\"</code>)</li>\n<li><strong>Tags</strong> to filter the runs (<code>tags=[\"decision-tree\", \"baseline\"]</code>)</li>\n<li>A dictionary <code>config</code> containing all the hyperparameters (automatically logged)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li>Log of the data used (artifact of type <code>dataset</code>)\nOnce the preprocessed data is ready:</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Save them locally if not already done</li>\n<li>Create an artifact of type <code>dataset</code></li>\n<li>Add each relevant file (e.g., X_train.csv, y_test.csv)</li>\n<li>Log the artifact with <code>wandb.log_artifact(...)</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>\ud83d\udca1 This step allows for versioning and precisely retrieving the data used during training, even several weeks later.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>Model Training and Metrics Logging</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Use the loaded data to train the model</li>\n<li>Evaluate it and log the results in W&amp;B via <code>wandb.log()</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li>Model Backup and Logging (artifact <code>model</code>)</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Save the model locally (joblib, pickle, etc.)</li>\n<li>Create an artifact of type <code>model</code></li>\n<li>Add the file and log it</li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>(Optional) Advanced Visualizations\nWhen training a classification model, having a detailed visualization of its performance is very useful. Weights &amp; Biases provides a handy function dedicated to scikit-learn models, wandb.sklearn.plot_classifier(), which automatically generates several key plots:</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confusion Matrix</strong>: Shows the distribution of true classes vs predicted classes. Useful for understanding where the model makes mistakes.</li>\n<li><strong>ROC Curve</strong>: Visualizes the model's ability to distinguish between positive and negative classes at different threshold values.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature Importance</strong>: For models that support it (like Decision Trees), shows which variables had the most influence on the decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol start=\"6\">\n<li>Clean closure of the run</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Wait a few seconds to allow W&amp;B to collect system resources (CPU, memory, etc.) with <code>time.sleep()</code></li>\n<li>Cleanly finish with <code>wandb.finish()</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>By rigorously applying this methodology to each experiment, particularly the systematic configuration of artifacts for models and data, the tracking of hyperparameters, and the structuring of your scripts, you ensure complete traceability of the training conditions. This allows the entire team to easily follow the progress of the work, reproduce the results exactly, and collaborate smoothly around a well-documented common foundation. This methodological rigor also serves as an essential foundation to ensure the scientific reproducibility of your machine learning projects.</p>\n\n</div>\n        <script src=\"https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.3.1.slim.min.js\"\n            integrity=\"sha384-q8i/X+965DzO0rT7abK41JStQIAqVgRVzpbzo5smXKp4YfRvH+8abtTE1Pi6jizo\"\n            crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>\n        \n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/prism.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-RDQSW3KoqJMiX0L/UBgwBmH1EmRYp8LBOiLaA8rBHIy+7OGP/7Gxg8vbt8wG4ZYd29P0Fnoq6+LOytCqx3cyoQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/themes/prism-okaidia.min.css\" integrity=\"sha512-mIs9kKbaw6JZFfSuo+MovjU+Ntggfoj8RwAmJbVXQ5mkAX5LlgETQEweFPI18humSPHymTb5iikEOKWF7I8ncQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"\n/>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/line-numbers/prism-line-numbers.min.css\" integrity=\"sha512-cbQXwDFK7lj2Fqfkuxbo5iD1dSbLlJGXGpfTDqbggqjHJeyzx88I3rfwjS38WJag/ihH7lzuGlGHpDBymLirZQ==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"\n    referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" />\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/autoloader/prism-autoloader.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-fTl/qcO1VgvKtOMApX2PdZzkziyr2stM65GYPLGuYMnuMm1z2JLJG6XVU7C/mR+E7xBUqCivykuhlzfqxXBXbg==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/prism/1.28.0/plugins/line-numbers/prism-line-numbers.min.js\" integrity=\"sha512-BttltKXFyWnGZQcRWj6osIg7lbizJchuAMotOkdLxHxwt/Hyo+cl47bZU0QADg+Qt5DJwni3SbYGXeGMB5cBcw==\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\"></script>\n<script src=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/de_custom.js\"></script>\n<link href=\"https://assets-datascientest.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/de/de_custom.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n",
    
